From d4d7dd8d0bb5c94b7b40bf1b05633d8ebe578ce9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Arena Agent <agent@arena.ai>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 14:08:01 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 17/29] Trace the driver, not the frame counter: the adapter is
 ours, so 0.2.0 is built from source

Asked whether the driver could be repaired or improved, I checked what the package actually
contains, and it is not what I had assumed in either direction. The repo's
DroidDeck-Mali-G615-driver-0.1.0.zip is upstream's newest release (panvk-kbase-v0.1.0-rc2, 2026
Oct 8): its libpanvk_backend.so is byte-identical in size to the release's
libvulkan_panfrost-glibc-aarch64.so, and meta.json names the same tag. So there was no newer
driver to import, and the beta.12 recommended yesterday is four days OLDER than what is
installed. docs/gpu-safety.md and the progress log said otherwise; both are corrected, and the
release notes say plainly what the driver itself does not solve: it names a present-after-two-
frames hang as a known issue, does not support the display server this guest runs, and is not
the driver that was tested on this SoC.

What the package holds, though, is 248 lines of our own - libvulkan_droiddeck_mali.so, the ICD
adapter, with its source and MIT licence in the zip. It is not where the fault is: it wraps
property queries to expose a DRM render node for gamescope and strips that extension before the
backend sees it, and it is not in the present path at all. But it IS a shim in the dispatch path
of every call the session makes, which is the one place an APK can reach that answers the
question the whole investigation is missing. "vkQueueSubmit never returned" and "vkQueueSubmit
returned and its fence was never signalled" are indistinguishable from frame timings, and they
need opposite fixes; the kernel panic happens in the second case and not the first.

So 0.2.0 adds that, behind an env var, off by default, and it changes nothing when off: the
wrappers are not even handed out, which is asserted, by pointer, against the backend's own
dispatch. Each traced call writes "-> name" and then "<- name = result", fsynced, and the file
lands in the session folder because the adapter resolves a relative name against the
BL_DEBUG_DIR the app already exports - a driver package that writes wherever it likes is not one
anyone would install.

    drivers/mali-g615/
      adapter/droiddeck_mali_icd.c   vendored, patched; the source the zip only carried
      build.sh                       wraps an upstream EMULATOR zip into a package
      test/{mock_backend.c,test_adapter.c,run_adapter_tests.sh}
      licenses/, SOURCE.json, CORRESPONDING-SOURCE.json

    .github/workflows/mali-driver-package.yml   apt cross-gcc -> build.sh -> import-policy check
                                                -> release asset mali-driver-latest

The adapter test builds a fake PanVK backend that reports a Mali-G615 MC6 and can be told to
SIGKILL the process inside vkQueuePresentKHR; what it is really asserting is the property the
whole trace exists for - that the file's last line names the call that never came back. The
packaging check runs the app's own DriverArchivePolicy regexes against the artifact, since a
driver the phone refuses to import is worse than no artifact at all. The guard workflow now also
clones Vulkan-Headers and runs tools/test/run_probe_tests.sh, so the probe's hand-written structs
are checked against the real ABI and not against the mock that has been agreeing with them.

Co-authored-by: arena-agent <297053741+arena-agent@users.noreply.github.com>
